# Website Governance Implementation

## Objective

Create a repeatable process for maintaining website quality, accessibility, content accuracy, and search visibility after the initial optimization work.

Rather than treating the project as a one-time website cleanup, I developed a structured approach for identifying, evaluating, prioritizing, resolving, and measuring website issues.

---

## Governance Framework

The project combined established accessibility guidance, evaluation tools, and website auditing practices.

### ♿ Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1

WCAG 2.1 was used as the primary accessibility guideline for evaluating website content and identifying accessibility improvement opportunities.

The guidelines provide testable success criteria for making web content more accessible to people with disabilities. They include three levels of conformance: A, AA, and AAA.

🔗 [W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2.1](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/)

### 🔎 WAVE Web Accessibility Evaluation Tool

WAVE was used to evaluate website pages for accessibility-related issues and identify areas requiring further review.

WAVE was used as an evaluation aid rather than as a certification mechanism. Automated tools can identify many accessibility issues, but human evaluation is still necessary to determine whether content is actually accessible.

🔗 [WAVE Web Accessibility Evaluation Tools](https://wave.webaim.org/)

### 🕷️ Screaming Frog SEO Spider

Screaming Frog SEO Spider was used to crawl and audit website pages for SEO and technical discoverability issues.

It supported the identification of issues such as page-level SEO opportunities, metadata problems, and other factors affecting search visibility.

🔗 [Screaming Frog SEO Spider](https://www.screamingfrog.co.uk/seo-spider/)

---

## Governance Process

The project followed a repeatable improvement cycle:

**Identify → Evaluate → Categorize → Prioritize → Resolve → Measure → Review**

### 1. Identify

Website pages were reviewed using accessibility tools, SEO auditing tools, and manual review.

### 2. Evaluate

Identified issues were evaluated against the appropriate framework or quality standard.

### 3. Categorize

Issues were organized into categories such as:

- Content
- Accessibility
- Readability
- SEO / Discoverability
- Other

### 4. Prioritize

Issues were prioritized based on their impact on accuracy, usability, accessibility, and discoverability.

### 5. Resolve

Applicable issues were corrected through CMS editing, content updates, SEO optimization, and accessibility improvements.

### 6. Measure

A KPI dashboard was created to track issue completion and performance improvements.

### 7. Review

The governance model supports continued review rather than treating website optimization as a one-time project.

---

## Governance & Measurement

The KPI dashboard provided visibility into project progress and allowed website improvements to be tracked quantitatively.

### Project Results

- **84 issues identified**
- **80 issues completed**
- **4 issues in progress**
- **5 pages audited**
- **16% average SEO improvement**

The dashboard also tracked issue completion by category, including content, accessibility, readability, and other website quality issues.

---

## Scope

The governance and remediation work focused on frontend/CMS editing, website content, accessibility, SEO, and structured website review.

The project did not include backend development or code-level remediation outside of the available CMS environment.
